  "terminal_reason": "completed",
  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule exist, and the rule is enabled. It has already run once with status `ok`.\n\n**1. What the data holds:** The `kibana_sample_data_logs` index is web-server access logs, with `timestamp` as the time field. Each entry has the response code, bytes sent, URL, client IP, source and destination country, OS, browser, file extension, referrer and tags. The data runs from 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03, so part of it is dated in the future. There are three response codes:\n\n| Code | Requests | Average bytes |\n|---|---|---|\n| 200 | 12,832 | about 5.9 KB |\n| 404 | 801 | about 5.0 KB |\n| 503 | 441 | 0 |\n\nThe response code is stored as text, not a number.\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-4c66e12d Web logs overview\":** It has seven panels and opens on the last 7 days:\n- Three single-number panels: total requests, unique visitors (by client IP) and total bytes served\n- A bar chart of requests over time, split by response code\n- A pie chart of response codes\n- A table of the top 10 URLs by requests and bytes\n- A bar chart of the top 10 source countries\n\nLink: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-4c66e12d-web-logs-overview-284e63b2b962\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-4c66e12d 5xx spike\"** (id `939b611f-de93-4ca0-907e-72b554bc1bbf`): It runs every minute and fires when more than 10 requests with a 5xx code arrive within 5 minutes. Because the code is text, the rule matches any code starting with \"5\". I checked that this filter finds exactly the 441 requests with code 503.\n\nThe rule has no notification attached, so when it fires the alert only shows up in Kibana. If you want it to send messages to Slack, email or similar, a connector needs to be added.",
